    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here is a table showing the Richter ratings for displacements recorded by a seismograph 100 km from the epicenter of an earthquake.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Compare an earthquake rated with a magnitude of 5 on the Richter scale and that rated with a 6. How do their displacements compare? What about an earthquake with a magnitude rated with a 2 and that rated with a 3?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Discuss with a partner how the displacement might be related to the Richter scale. Express that relationship in words or with an expression.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "An earthquake shook the northwest part of Indonesia in 2004, causing massive damage and casualties. If a seismograph was located 100 km from the epicenter, it would have recorded a displacement of 125 m! Use your answer to the previous…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Logarithms are helpful in a variety of real-world contexts. Let’s look at an example in chemistry.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The acidity of a substance is measured by the concentration of positive hydrogen ions, \\(H\\), in moles per liter. If the concentration is \\(10^{x}\\), then the acidity rating, or pH rating, is \\(\\text{-}x\\). For example, grapefruit juice…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can see that the pH rating is -1 times the exponent in the expression representing the hydrogen ion concentration. Because the exponent in a power of 10 can be expressed in terms of the base 10 logarithm, the pH rating can be expressed…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "When the exponent in a power of 10 increases by 1, say from \\(10^\\text{-3}\\) to \\(10^\\text{-2}\\), the quantity changes by a factor of 10. This means that lemon juice has 10 times the hydrogen ion concentration of grapefruit juice. Water,…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another example of logarithm use is the Richter scale, which measures the strength of an earthquake in terms of the displacement of the needle on a seismograph. A displacement of 1 micrometer, one millionth of a meter, measures 1 on the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "If the seismograph displacement is \\(d\\) meters, the Richter rating of the earthquake can be expressed as \\(7 +\\log_{10}{d}\\). We can check that when \\(d = 1 \\times 10^{\\text-6}\\) (a displacement of 1 micrometer), the Richter rating is 1.…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A logarithmic function is a constant multiple of a logarithm to some base, so it is a function given by \\(f(x) = k \\log_{a}(x)\\) where \\(k\\) is any number and \\(a\\) is a positive number (10, 2, or \\(e\\) in this course). The graph of a…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L18-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "18.4: Measuring Earthquake Strength",
      "SourceExcerpt": "logarithmic function",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l18.rich-lesson-v2.json",
